Agreed.

I think the source-material separation is materially stronger after the extraction-survival additions.

The distinction I wanted was:

- project state persists
- source files rehydrate

What v6 adds is the missing durability requirement:

- meaning must survive even when the source file does not

I agree with:

- Tier D (rehydrated sources)
- extraction survival
- artifact/source classification
- required_attachments
- visual-source extraction
- bootstrap attachment handling
- no phantom attachments
- no invented filenames

The artifact versus source distinction feels especially important.

My preferred interpretation remains:

If the assistant produced, edited, versioned, rewrote, or transformed it, it travels as an artifact regardless of how it entered the project.

The extraction-survival rule also resolves my biggest concern with file rehydration.

A ledger item, artifact, or next step should never depend on content that exists only inside a dropped attachment.

The payload must remain self-explanatory even before files are re-uploaded.

I also agree with keeping required_attachments as a flat list.

Names only.

The user generally knows their files already, and the purpose of the list is inventory rather than explanation.

The restoration behaviour also feels correct:

- restore
- state next step
- identify missing attachments
- identify which are relevant to the pending step
- wait

and if instructed to proceed without a file, proceed while flagging affected areas rather than refusing.

At this point I do not see any conceptual issues with the source-material boundary.

My inclination is to treat v6 as the candidate test version and move into the T1–T9 protocol rather than continuing architecture work.

T7 and T8 in particular appear likely to reveal whether the boundary behaves as intended in practice.
